{"connection_id": "BC-5c651400a9aad71599b5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.14.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pursue peace and mutual upbuilding", "note": "Paul joins the pursuit of peace to building one another up; this closely matches the commentary's reciprocal movement from contested truth to mutual counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f25af914c16e8da4e80", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.14.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pursue peace and mutual upbuilding", "note": "The commentary makes each believer responsible for another's progress; Paul calls them to pursue what makes for peace and builds one another up amid disputes over conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd8f267a5e756413aaf7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.14.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eater and abstainer must not despise or judge", "note": "Paul names both sides of the practice dispute and says God has welcomed each; this directly checks mutual claims of moral superiority."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b05a144908db83c69673", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.15.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "instruct one another", "note": "The commentary stresses that each person both gives and receives counsel; Paul says the Romans are full of goodness and knowledge and able to admonish one another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c5be8d70864ccb7fa65", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.15.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "welcome one another as Christ welcomed you", "note": "The community's welcome is grounded in the welcome it has received; this makes inclusion reciprocal rather than a claim held by one side."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ada9d7c34cc11aeb0662", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.15.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "welcome one another", "note": "The commentary's estranged parties become companions; Paul commands mutual welcome as Christ has welcomed them, giving the bond a concrete reception practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18362466c242cdc83247", "ref": "SBLGNT:Titus.3.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "warn then separate from divisive person", "note": "The commentary emphasizes holding a group together through counsel; Titus directs warning a divisive person twice and then having nothing further to do with him, marking a limit to maintained companionship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69d1fc1241c2afc44775", "ref": "SBLGNT:Titus.3.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "avoid quarreling and be gentle", "note": "The commentary's reconciliation changes how people address one another; Titus orders believers to avoid quarrels and show gentleness to all people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1bdceb3cd1b9ff719f4e", "ref": "Avot de-Rabbi Natan A 12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Aaron reconciles two disputants through personal messages", "note": "The tradition depicts Aaron telling each disputant that the other regrets the quarrel until they meet and embrace; it turns the commentary's mutual counsel into a specific practice of restoring bonds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cae61764fb23203bec53", "ref": "Avot de-Rabbi Nathan A 12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Aaron reconciles quarreling people", "note": "The commentary's two disputants need an action that reconnects them; this elaboration of Aaron as peacemaker depicts him approaching each estranged party to bring about reunion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-891912e0aa942f4e984b", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Bava Metzia 30b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jerusalem destroyed for insisting on strict law", "note": "The Talmud interprets the city's destruction as a failure to go beyond the letter of law; this provides a Jewish counter-reading in which claiming formal right without generosity cannot sustain peace."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b095f7a0474c1261ec95", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Bava Metzia 84a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "challenges between learning companions", "note": "The commentary's reciprocal truth requires a giver and receiver; Rabbi Yohanan mourns Resh Lakish's challenging objections because their exchange developed the teaching, unlike a companion who only confirms him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e3bb887470eff12c4a1f", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Berakhot 64a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "scholars increase peace in the world", "note": "The Talmud reads the scriptural promise of peace through the work of scholars; this links the commentary's mutual counsel to peace-making, though by an interpretive bridge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93eb17ad9c002e4e8ba6", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Eruvin 13b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hillel's school presents Shammai's view before its own", "note": "The schools' dispute remains a shared search, and Hillel's humility and fair teaching explain why his view prevails; this gives the commentary's opposing claims a model of argument that preserves relationship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4b076bb738c83b50cbbc", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Eruvin 13b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Hillel teaches opponents' words first", "note": "The commentary's reciprocal truth requires receiving as well as giving; the houses dispute law, but Hillel's house wins praise for humility and teaching Shammai's view before its own."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71ef7db70735bfb8c568", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Kiddushin 30b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "study dispute ends in love", "note": "The commentary's two sides can transmit truth and still become companions; this interpretation pictures father and son or teacher and pupil contending fiercely in Torah study yet parting as loving friends."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f76cf8b35e6c80f3daa", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Sanhedrin 6b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "compromise as judgment of peace", "note": "The commentary's rivals both assert right, yet peace changes the result; the rabbinic reading of judgment of peace in Zechariah 8:16 identifies compromise as a way to join adjudication and reconciliation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-368639dbbb4f2ded9859", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 127a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bringing peace between a person and a fellow", "note": "The Talmud names interpersonal peacemaking among deeds rewarded in this world; this gives the commentary's sulh a concrete ethical practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f1f9ffc5402fbce152d", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 127a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "make peace between people", "note": "The commentary treats reconciliation as work among opposed people; the rabbinic list of deeds whose benefits extend into the world to come includes making peace between one person and another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a00bdb47e2032f3aa759", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 31a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the neighbor treated by the golden rule", "note": "Hillel summarizes Torah through the instruction not to do to another what one hates; this makes mutual regard the test for how one treats a fellow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc3c9c0eb4a40a5ebf27", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Shevuot 39a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Israel responsible for one another", "note": "The dictum that all Israel are guarantors for one another states the mutual obligation implicit when the commentary says each gives and receives counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee40250ca6f4c8e03e58", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Ta'anit 22a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "jesters cheer the sad and reconcile quarrelers", "note": "The two men describe both lifting the downcast and making peace between disputants; their paired actions join companionship in loss to active reconciliation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e9938309ada64f956f4", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Ta'anit 23a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "companionship or death", "note": "The commentary contrasts the human alone in loss with the belonging of companions; the Honi story ends with a scholar unable to find companions and a saying that one needs fellowship or death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8fe3b3d38e2d72408fd", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Yevamot 14b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hillel and Shammai preserve marriages despite legal disagreement", "note": "The schools disagree about rulings yet do not refuse intermarriage; this shows opposing claims coexisting without severing kinship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1cf0d90339eea7dc0be", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Yevamot 14b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "marriage despite legal disagreement", "note": "The commentary's parties can disagree yet preserve kinship; the houses of Shammai and Hillel did not refrain from marrying one another despite their disputes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-148ea39ecfcbe9bda488", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Yevamot 65b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "alter words for peace", "note": "The commentary binds haqq and sulh together; the rabbinic permission to change words for peace presses the question of how truthful speech serves a repaired relationship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f010bd8cc46c16295cb3", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Yoma 9b", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "baseless hatred brings the Second Temple's destruction", "note": "The Talmud explains a communal catastrophe through hatred among people; this makes estrangement itself a source of collective loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89dcf7af7d75a53519e8", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 8:5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Truth and Peace contest creating humanity", "note": "The commentary's human being is threatened by contention while truth and peace must be joined; this midrash personifies Truth and Peace as disputing whether humanity should be created, because humans lie and quarrel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd9ea4805f6e2d645f6e", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Aaron loves peace and brings people near Torah", "note": "The Mishnah interprets peacemaking as active pursuit joined to love of people and drawing them near; it expands the commentary's sulh from ending repulsion to restoring closeness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39ec10d6292febb70adf", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Aaron pursues peace", "note": "The commentary treats peace as ending estrangement and drawing people into closeness; Hillel's maxim to be Aaron's disciple joins loving and pursuing peace with loving people and drawing them near Torah."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc496167f39b60f5a8a5", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "if only for myself, what am I", "note": "The commentary's solitary human cannot escape loss through self-concern; Hillel's question makes the inadequacy of living only for oneself explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a809e5859994c64b0d01", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:18", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "justice, truth, and peace sustain the world", "note": "The Mishnah places justice, truth, and peace together as the world's supports; this gives a compact ethical frame for the commentary's claims of haqq, peace, and communal relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8737ae375b551686b499", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 2:4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stay with the community; do not judge before knowing one's place", "note": "The Mishnah warns against separation from the community and premature judgment of another; this answers the commentary's isolated human and the disputants each sure of their own right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d26704552f75be09c1a6", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 2:4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "do not separate from community", "note": "The commentary contrasts the solitary human with a believing company; Hillel's instruction not to separate oneself from the community treats continued belonging as a deliberate duty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5ee511bfd19a037fd027", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "divine presence rests where two discuss Torah", "note": "The Mishnah makes shared study a sacred meeting between people; it gives the commentary's reciprocal counsel a setting in which relationship itself matters."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b6cd0f7d9dc061d6cdc", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wisdom comes from learning from every person", "note": "Ben Zoma defines the wise person as one who learns from everyone; this makes the commentary's reciprocal relation a source of wisdom rather than solitary possession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-26b4a4c6360d0a094954", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 5:17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "disputes for Heaven's sake endure", "note": "The Mishnah contrasts enduring disputes for Heaven, exemplified by Hillel and Shammai, with a dispute that does not endure; it distinguishes principled disagreement from contention that destroys bonds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f954e4cf799bc268d7ca", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 5:17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "dispute for heaven's sake", "note": "The commentary initially places claim against claim, then makes truth serve the bond; this maxim distinguishes principled Hillel-Shammai disagreement from Korah's dispute, qualifying any simple condemnation of disagreement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-27d2baa3c0da5943ea94", "ref": "Mishnah Peah 1:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "peace between people as a deed with lasting fruit", "note": "The Mishnah explicitly lists making peace between one person and another among deeds whose fruit is enjoyed in this world; it links the commentary's good action to repaired relations."}

