{"connection_id": "BC-fc18166c3734de46d859", "ref": "Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one human ancestor taught as a basis for peace among creatures", "note": "The Mishnah says humanity descends from one person so no one can claim a superior ancestry; this directly counters rival claims and grounds common human dignity in kinship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f179e15e5e809ca109c0", "ref": "Sifra, Kedoshim 4:12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "neighbor-love as a great principle of Torah", "note": "Rabbi Akiva reads love of one's neighbor as a great principle; this interpretation makes the commentary's intimacy and mutual care a frame for the wider law."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92edb6af7933739df897", "ref": "Sirach 28:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forgive a neighbor before praying for forgiveness", "note": "Ben Sira makes forgiveness of another the condition mirrored in one's own prayer; this gives the commentary's repair of hostility a reciprocal religious practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b25bf0d3334260d837a", "ref": "Sirach 28:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forgive neighbor's wrong", "note": "The commentary's rival claims need the grievance to end so the bond can resume; Sirach commands forgiveness of a neighbor's wrong and connects that act with receiving pardon in prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a22448edd5944abd532a", "ref": "Sirach 28:8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "avoid strife because anger kindles it", "note": "The sage says restraint from quarrel reduces sin and a hot-tempered person ignites conflict; this closely joins patience with the prevention of strife."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d92929454cf2a6c5bb0e", "ref": "Sirach 6:14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a faithful friend is a sturdy shelter and a treasure", "note": "Ben Sira gives the close friend a protective role; this concretizes the commentary's intimate bond as refuge in human vulnerability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6dbce92f38375abf563f", "ref": "Sirach 6:14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faithful friend as shelter", "note": "The commentary's close friend gives security after frightened separation; Sirach calls a faithful friend a sturdy shelter and a treasure, making companionship protective."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a16b37b90cdfa86e8ebc", "ref": "Sirach 6:16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a faithful friend is life-saving medicine", "note": "The friend is described as healing support found by those who fear God; this adds the companion's restorative effect to the commentary's account of connection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d560dbc7349e7216e7bd", "ref": "Testament of Gad 6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "peaceful speech to someone who sinned", "note": "The commentary's counsel repairs a bond after wrongdoing; this testament urges love among brothers and peaceful speech to someone who sins against a companion. The exact verse division within chapter 6 is uncertain from memory."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d1038fab728fc656f24", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.12.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rejected counsel ends in the people's division", "note": "After Rehoboam rejects the elders' advice, Israel refuses his rule and separates; this shows the social loss that follows counsel answered with hardness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6da3628545f988abbbe", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.12.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "elders advise the king to serve and answer gently", "note": "The elders tell Rehoboam that serving the people and speaking good words will win their service; this makes reciprocal regard a practical alternative to coercive rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80a0b0c7c3ab63ababbf", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.18.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jonathan's soul bound to David", "note": "The commentary names affection and kinship as bonds among close friends; Jonathan's soul is knit to David's and he loves David as himself, giving narrative form to chosen attachment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-385a700feb24feb5efa4", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.20.42", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "peace and enduring friendship covenant", "note": "The commentary's close companions remain connected through difficulty; Jonathan sends David away in peace under a covenant binding them and their descendants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76641c2d33546e1e2516", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.25.33", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abigail's counsel keeps David from bloodguilt", "note": "David blesses Abigail for restraining him from revenge; her wise intervention makes counsel the means by which a violent conflict is prevented."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ffa5e69b6af612f34ed4", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.28.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "captives treated as kin and returned", "note": "Israelite leaders clothe, feed, and transport Judean captives back to their kin; care across a hostile conflict makes the commentary's removal of estrangement tangible."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8b910aecc274b15bddb", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.6.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "captured enemies fed and released", "note": "Elisha has the Aramean captives fed before they return home, after which their raiding bands stop; hospitality changes the enemy relation that the commentary says peace can transform."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2979e85bbbcf1b352af1", "ref": "WLC:Amos.5.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع ص ر / Hebrew עצר (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary joins good action with communal attachment; God rejects Israel's festal assemblies in Amos, with Hebrew עצר, making mere gathering insufficient for the commentary's ethical togetherness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-895f0444f79269ce0a63", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no human on earth can answer the king", "note": "The commentary labels الإنسان under ء ن س and places the human in loss; the Aramaic verse says no human can meet the impossible demand, then Daniel turns to companions, making the following shared appeal a pointed contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1da09d9def33af2cf406", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic אנש, human unable to answer alone", "note": "The commentary labels الإنسان under ء ن س; the table lists Aramaic אנש only as an unverified sound correspondence, and this verse uses it for a human no one on earth can find capable of the task."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d678edd91b94a5829215", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Daniel brings his companions into the crisis", "note": "Daniel tells Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah what threatens him and asks them to seek mercy together; shared appeal replaces the impossible task facing a human alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2974e7b20d639e7c738", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س / Aramaic אנש (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary begins with the human in loss; Daniel's vision presents a human-like figure coming with heaven's clouds, an exalted use of Aramaic enash. The roots may correspond, but the figure is not the commentary's ordinary solitary person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-098842c4cee950dffe4d", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "burdens and disputes not borne alone", "note": "Moses says he cannot bear the people's troubles, burdens, and disputes alone; the commentary likewise moves from the solitary human in loss to mutual counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b63830f723184e5554e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wise people appointed to judge", "note": "After naming the people's disputes as too much for one person, Moses asks them to select wise and discerning leaders; this distributes the work of judging competing claims."}
{"connection_id": "BC-41c4834b1f18da332fcc", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hear brothers and judge fairly", "note": "The commentary begins in opposed claims and seeks a shared right; this instruction requires judges to hear disputes between brothers and judge rightly, supplying a social practice for adjudication."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c10e67e3b42d2655fc75", "ref": "WLC:Deut.15.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "open hand to poor brother", "note": "The commentary's negative example is failure to urge feeding the needy; this command says to open one's hand to the poor and needy brother in the land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9227260487c125ab1cb8", "ref": "WLC:Deut.16.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "עצרת, a gathered festival assembly", "note": "The commentary glosses its Arabic root label ع ص ر as a bond of affection or kinship; the table gives Hebrew עצר as a sound correspondence, and Deuteronomy's ʿăṣārâ names a public assembly, a related gathering image with a distinct sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28696351ef9eb24afc85", "ref": "WLC:Deut.16.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ʿăṣārâ, assembly; table sound correspondence only", "note": "The commentary's gloss of Arabic ع ص ر concerns absent affection or kinship, while this Hebrew form means a festival assembly; the possible link is only the shared gathering image, not an established shared sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17677c8586f40b89ee0c", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "disputants heard and judged", "note": "When two people have a dispute, judges decide between them and distinguish the righteous from the guilty; this locates contested right in a hearing rather than either side's assertion alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e2767b8e5b9964e27981", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "litigants and justified party", "note": "The opening disputants both claim haqq; this law places two disputants before judges who vindicate the righteous and condemn the wicked, keeping a verdict distinct from rival assertions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70714d8999749696d225", "ref": "WLC:Deut.6.20", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חקק and ḥuqqim, prescribed statutes", "note": "The commentary labels haqq under ح ق ق and says it passes between people; the table offers Hebrew חקק as an unverified sound correspondence, and the child's question about statutes begins an intergenerational transmission of prescribed teaching rather than a litigant's claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc5d9994020a268aa16f", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one raises a fallen companion", "note": "Ecclesiastes calls the solitary fallen person wretched because no one is there to lift him; the companion's action directly answers the commentary's human alone in loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b05acf873b0da3e257b4", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one companion lifts the fallen", "note": "The commentary's companions give and receive aid; Ecclesiastes says one can lift the other when he falls, whereas the solitary person has no one to lift him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0dc6c88f0c591786212", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a shared cord resists being broken", "note": "Ecclesiastes says two withstand attack and a threefold cord is not quickly torn; this pictures the resilience of joined people, extending the commentary's bond image beyond counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4aa311eb1f17add0e4fa", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "companionship resists attack", "note": "The commentary says quarrelling dissipates a group's strength; Ecclesiastes says one person may be overpowered but two can stand against an attacker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79bf6cad6ba556b96180", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two have return for work", "note": "The commentary contrasts the solitary human in loss with a mutually helpful company; Ecclesiastes says two are better than one because they gain a good return for their labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fe4beec76faa8f9808b5", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "return an enemy's lost animal", "note": "The commentary's enemy-to-friend turn takes patient action; the law orders one who meets an enemy's wandering ox or donkey to bring it back to him, acting for an adversary's good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4734bb9d47ebfd89e992", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "help one who hates you", "note": "The commentary's former opponents can stand beside one another; the law orders help for the burdened donkey of someone who hates you, turning estrangement into practical cooperation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f14e3bb5810ffead4210", "ref": "WLC:Exod.33.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses speaks with God as with a friend", "note": "The commentary develops the close-friend sense of its insan word-family; Exodus uses friendship for face-to-face intimacy, an analogue that is explicitly divine-human rather than between peers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-560e42a750a3c454531d", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.22", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two kingdoms made one people", "note": "Ezekiel says divided Israel and Judah will no longer be two nations but one under one king; this pictures separated groups joined into one community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-57362345d682a92f1980", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.6.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ص ل ح / Aramaic צלח (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary's salihat are deeds performed by the believing group; Ezra's elders prosper as they build together under prophetic encouragement, using the proposed Aramaic root. Prosperous progress is a different sense from reconciliation."}

