{"connection_id": "BC-fc18166c3734de46d859", "ref": "Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one human ancestor taught as a basis for peace among creatures", "note": "The Mishnah says humanity descends from one person so no one can claim a superior ancestry; this directly counters rival claims and grounds common human dignity in kinship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f179e15e5e809ca109c0", "ref": "Sifra, Kedoshim 4:12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "neighbor-love as a great principle of Torah", "note": "Rabbi Akiva reads love of one's neighbor as a great principle; this interpretation makes the commentary's intimacy and mutual care a frame for the wider law."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92edb6af7933739df897", "ref": "Sirach 28:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forgive a neighbor before praying for forgiveness", "note": "Ben Sira makes forgiveness of another the condition mirrored in one's own prayer; this gives the commentary's repair of hostility a reciprocal religious practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b25bf0d3334260d837a", "ref": "Sirach 28:2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forgive neighbor's wrong", "note": "The commentary's rival claims need the grievance to end so the bond can resume; Sirach commands forgiveness of a neighbor's wrong and connects that act with receiving pardon in prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a22448edd5944abd532a", "ref": "Sirach 28:8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "avoid strife because anger kindles it", "note": "The sage says restraint from quarrel reduces sin and a hot-tempered person ignites conflict; this closely joins patience with the prevention of strife."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d92929454cf2a6c5bb0e", "ref": "Sirach 6:14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a faithful friend is a sturdy shelter and a treasure", "note": "Ben Sira gives the close friend a protective role; this concretizes the commentary's intimate bond as refuge in human vulnerability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6dbce92f38375abf563f", "ref": "Sirach 6:14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faithful friend as shelter", "note": "The commentary's close friend gives security after frightened separation; Sirach calls a faithful friend a sturdy shelter and a treasure, making companionship protective."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a16b37b90cdfa86e8ebc", "ref": "Sirach 6:16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a faithful friend is life-saving medicine", "note": "The friend is described as healing support found by those who fear God; this adds the companion's restorative effect to the commentary's account of connection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d560dbc7349e7216e7bd", "ref": "Testament of Gad 6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "peaceful speech to someone who sinned", "note": "The commentary's counsel repairs a bond after wrongdoing; this testament urges love among brothers and peaceful speech to someone who sins against a companion. The exact verse division within chapter 6 is uncertain from memory."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d1038fab728fc656f24", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.12.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rejected counsel ends in the people's division", "note": "After Rehoboam rejects the elders' advice, Israel refuses his rule and separates; this shows the social loss that follows counsel answered with hardness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6da3628545f988abbbe", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.12.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "elders advise the king to serve and answer gently", "note": "The elders tell Rehoboam that serving the people and speaking good words will win their service; this makes reciprocal regard a practical alternative to coercive rule."}
