{"connection_id": "BC-8d1038fab728fc656f24", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.12.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rejected counsel ends in the people's division", "note": "After Rehoboam rejects the elders' advice, Israel refuses his rule and separates; this shows the social loss that follows counsel answered with hardness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6da3628545f988abbbe", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.12.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "elders advise the king to serve and answer gently", "note": "The elders tell Rehoboam that serving the people and speaking good words will win their service; this makes reciprocal regard a practical alternative to coercive rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80a0b0c7c3ab63ababbf", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.18.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jonathan's soul bound to David", "note": "The commentary names affection and kinship as bonds among close friends; Jonathan's soul is knit to David's and he loves David as himself, giving narrative form to chosen attachment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-385a700feb24feb5efa4", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.20.42", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "peace and enduring friendship covenant", "note": "The commentary's close companions remain connected through difficulty; Jonathan sends David away in peace under a covenant binding them and their descendants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76641c2d33546e1e2516", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.25.33", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abigail's counsel keeps David from bloodguilt", "note": "David blesses Abigail for restraining him from revenge; her wise intervention makes counsel the means by which a violent conflict is prevented."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ffa5e69b6af612f34ed4", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.28.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "captives treated as kin and returned", "note": "Israelite leaders clothe, feed, and transport Judean captives back to their kin; care across a hostile conflict makes the commentary's removal of estrangement tangible."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8b910aecc274b15bddb", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.6.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "captured enemies fed and released", "note": "Elisha has the Aramean captives fed before they return home, after which their raiding bands stop; hospitality changes the enemy relation that the commentary says peace can transform."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2979e85bbbcf1b352af1", "ref": "WLC:Amos.5.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع ص ر / Hebrew עצר (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary joins good action with communal attachment; God rejects Israel's festal assemblies in Amos, with Hebrew עצר, making mere gathering insufficient for the commentary's ethical togetherness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-895f0444f79269ce0a63", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no human on earth can answer the king", "note": "The commentary labels الإنسان under ء ن س and places the human in loss; the Aramaic verse says no human can meet the impossible demand, then Daniel turns to companions, making the following shared appeal a pointed contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1da09d9def33af2cf406", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic אנש, human unable to answer alone", "note": "The commentary labels الإنسان under ء ن س; the table lists Aramaic אנש only as an unverified sound correspondence, and this verse uses it for a human no one on earth can find capable of the task."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d678edd91b94a5829215", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Daniel brings his companions into the crisis", "note": "Daniel tells Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah what threatens him and asks them to seek mercy together; shared appeal replaces the impossible task facing a human alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2974e7b20d639e7c738", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س / Aramaic אנש (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary begins with the human in loss; Daniel's vision presents a human-like figure coming with heaven's clouds, an exalted use of Aramaic enash. The roots may correspond, but the figure is not the commentary's ordinary solitary person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-098842c4cee950dffe4d", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "burdens and disputes not borne alone", "note": "Moses says he cannot bear the people's troubles, burdens, and disputes alone; the commentary likewise moves from the solitary human in loss to mutual counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b63830f723184e5554e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wise people appointed to judge", "note": "After naming the people's disputes as too much for one person, Moses asks them to select wise and discerning leaders; this distributes the work of judging competing claims."}
{"connection_id": "BC-41c4834b1f18da332fcc", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hear brothers and judge fairly", "note": "The commentary begins in opposed claims and seeks a shared right; this instruction requires judges to hear disputes between brothers and judge rightly, supplying a social practice for adjudication."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c10e67e3b42d2655fc75", "ref": "WLC:Deut.15.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "open hand to poor brother", "note": "The commentary's negative example is failure to urge feeding the needy; this command says to open one's hand to the poor and needy brother in the land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9227260487c125ab1cb8", "ref": "WLC:Deut.16.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "עצרת, a gathered festival assembly", "note": "The commentary glosses its Arabic root label ع ص ر as a bond of affection or kinship; the table gives Hebrew עצר as a sound correspondence, and Deuteronomy's ʿăṣārâ names a public assembly, a related gathering image with a distinct sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28696351ef9eb24afc85", "ref": "WLC:Deut.16.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ʿăṣārâ, assembly; table sound correspondence only", "note": "The commentary's gloss of Arabic ع ص ر concerns absent affection or kinship, while this Hebrew form means a festival assembly; the possible link is only the shared gathering image, not an established shared sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17677c8586f40b89ee0c", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "disputants heard and judged", "note": "When two people have a dispute, judges decide between them and distinguish the righteous from the guilty; this locates contested right in a hearing rather than either side's assertion alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e2767b8e5b9964e27981", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "litigants and justified party", "note": "The opening disputants both claim haqq; this law places two disputants before judges who vindicate the righteous and condemn the wicked, keeping a verdict distinct from rival assertions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70714d8999749696d225", "ref": "WLC:Deut.6.20", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חקק and ḥuqqim, prescribed statutes", "note": "The commentary labels haqq under ح ق ق and says it passes between people; the table offers Hebrew חקק as an unverified sound correspondence, and the child's question about statutes begins an intergenerational transmission of prescribed teaching rather than a litigant's claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc5d9994020a268aa16f", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one raises a fallen companion", "note": "Ecclesiastes calls the solitary fallen person wretched because no one is there to lift him; the companion's action directly answers the commentary's human alone in loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b05acf873b0da3e257b4", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one companion lifts the fallen", "note": "The commentary's companions give and receive aid; Ecclesiastes says one can lift the other when he falls, whereas the solitary person has no one to lift him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0dc6c88f0c591786212", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a shared cord resists being broken", "note": "Ecclesiastes says two withstand attack and a threefold cord is not quickly torn; this pictures the resilience of joined people, extending the commentary's bond image beyond counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4aa311eb1f17add0e4fa", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "companionship resists attack", "note": "The commentary says quarrelling dissipates a group's strength; Ecclesiastes says one person may be overpowered but two can stand against an attacker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79bf6cad6ba556b96180", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two have return for work", "note": "The commentary contrasts the solitary human in loss with a mutually helpful company; Ecclesiastes says two are better than one because they gain a good return for their labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fe4beec76faa8f9808b5", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "return an enemy's lost animal", "note": "The commentary's enemy-to-friend turn takes patient action; the law orders one who meets an enemy's wandering ox or donkey to bring it back to him, acting for an adversary's good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4734bb9d47ebfd89e992", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "help one who hates you", "note": "The commentary's former opponents can stand beside one another; the law orders help for the burdened donkey of someone who hates you, turning estrangement into practical cooperation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f14e3bb5810ffead4210", "ref": "WLC:Exod.33.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses speaks with God as with a friend", "note": "The commentary develops the close-friend sense of its insan word-family; Exodus uses friendship for face-to-face intimacy, an analogue that is explicitly divine-human rather than between peers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-560e42a750a3c454531d", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.22", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two kingdoms made one people", "note": "Ezekiel says divided Israel and Judah will no longer be two nations but one under one king; this pictures separated groups joined into one community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-57362345d682a92f1980", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.6.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ص ل ح / Aramaic צלח (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary's salihat are deeds performed by the believing group; Ezra's elders prosper as they build together under prophetic encouragement, using the proposed Aramaic root. Prosperous progress is a different sense from reconciliation."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-034ff7f52d69d483451a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.11.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "people speak to one another to build together", "note": "The repeated mutual speech coordinates Babel's brickmaking; unlike the commentary's counsel toward haqq, this is a counter-example of solidarity directed toward a self-exalting project."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3302c06b5fb82c0bf5fa", "ref": "WLC:Gen.11.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shared speech is confused so people cannot understand one another", "note": "The Babel scene reverses the commentary's image of people passing counsel between them: collective speech becomes unintelligible and the group is broken apart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f91fc1b98a35779f96b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.13.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strife stopped because the parties are kin", "note": "Abram asks that there be no strife between him and Lot because they are brothers; kinship removes the repulsion that the commentary says sulh repairs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8555f1988bc58145e267", "ref": "WLC:Gen.13.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strife between kin", "note": "Abram asks Lot to end the quarrel between their herdsmen because they are brothers; it gives a concrete family scene for replacing contention with an acknowledged bond."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7578273c32480b027f2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Esau embraces the brother he had opposed", "note": "Esau runs to Jacob, embraces him, and they weep; the estranged brothers' bodily welcome gives a particular scene of hostility yielding to closeness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13aa0e94d15b7329c278", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Esau embraces Jacob", "note": "The commentary's enemy becomes a close companion; after Jacob fears Esau, Esau runs to embrace and kiss him, giving a narrative instance of hostile distance becoming kinship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f52ba1225b3cb0dc4f63", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brothers cannot speak peaceably", "note": "The commentary begins with estrangement that peace must remove; Joseph's brothers hate him and cannot speak to him in peace, making the broken bond audible in their speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d97473cd8755e10388e7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "am I my brother's keeper", "note": "The commentary answers the solitary human's loss through responsibility for companions; Cain evades responsibility for his murdered brother with this question, giving the stark opposite of mutual care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f0f0bc14aa08d46c20d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joseph embraces and talks with brothers", "note": "The commentary's separated kin are joined through interpersonal exchange; after revealing himself, Joseph kisses his brothers and they speak with him, reversing their earlier alienation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-66a7672dd31af29c9d5f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joseph tells frightened brothers not to grieve over their betrayal", "note": "Joseph reframes their sale of him as God's means of preserving life; the speech releases his brothers from fear and turns a contested wrong toward reconciliation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20b1035bc2acd0585392", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brothers ask Joseph for forgiveness", "note": "The commentary's peace removes the fear that divided relatives; Joseph's brothers ask him to forgive their evil, and Joseph weeps when they speak, placing repaired kinship after wrongdoing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b32cbbb2f19c897bd7b6", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joseph comforts his brothers and speaks kindly", "note": "After the brothers fear revenge again, Joseph reassures and comforts them; this is a necessary continuation showing reconciliation maintained through compassionate speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9bdb2b5fa73431bb0a8e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "comfort and kindly speech after fear", "note": "The commentary's frightened separation becomes close companionship; Joseph tells his brothers not to fear, promises provision for their children, and speaks to their hearts after their plea for forgiveness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-42332aee8c1b2fa7c15e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.1.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع ص ر / Hebrew עצר (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary's asr names a bond whose absence is felt; Isaiah says God cannot bear wrongdoing joined to a solemn assembly, using Hebrew עצר. An assembled group does not guarantee the just relation the commentary seeks."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ceaa351fab67ef2adfed", "ref": "WLC:Isa.1.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seek justice and correct oppression", "note": "The commentary's right is to be practiced and shared, including care for the vulnerable; Isaiah demands learning good, seeking justice, correcting oppression and pleading for widow and orphan."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc366737d30e8e00fabc", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary's haqq is a shared right rather than a weapon of contention; Isaiah condemns those who decree unjust decrees using the proposed Hebrew root, showing that enacted rules can contradict right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4bb2fe3f520b5f6661c8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.19.25", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Egypt, Assyria, and Israel share a blessing", "note": "The prophet names former rival peoples together as God's people, handiwork, and inheritance; this extends the commentary's turn from hostility to kin-like belonging across nations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-201af0fd6ea97bcfdaff", "ref": "WLC:Isa.32.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteousness produces peace and quiet security", "note": "Isaiah presents peace and settled confidence as the effect of righteousness; this joins ethical rightness to social calm, though it does not describe a specific dispute."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a441e15314954d72b149", "ref": "WLC:Isa.32.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteousness makes peace", "note": "The commentary places good deeds beside shared truth and patience; this verse says the work of righteousness will be peace, making just action the condition of settled relations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1238b60ae0ada4d5233", "ref": "WLC:Isa.5.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perverted judgment despite claims", "note": "The commentary's two sides claiming right need a measure beyond assertion; Isaiah condemns those who justify the wicked for a bribe and deprive the righteous of right."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9171ef62a80193d7fe86", "ref": "WLC:Isa.51.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س / Hebrew אנש (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary's human being is vulnerable apart from the group; Isaiah asks why one fears mortal enosh who will die like grass. The table's proposed root gives a frailty comparison rather than the commentary's sense of intimacy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a138904ba4819bf34250", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "provide for the hungry", "note": "The commentary points to failure to urge feeding the poor as a defect in shared life; Isaiah requires giving oneself to the hungry and satisfying the afflicted person's need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ec24e873f695d48abdaf", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "share bread and do not hide from kin", "note": "The commentary's poor person needs active care, while affection preserves human bonds; Isaiah commands sharing bread with the hungry and not hiding from one's own flesh."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e02269fb282c668bc22", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remove oppressive gestures and speech", "note": "The commentary's communal peace requires a change from contention to good action; Isaiah links God's response to removing the yoke, threatening gesture and wicked speech from among the people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01f61c19e0f28e794481", "ref": "WLC:Jer.12.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary pairs salihat with faith and relational right; Jeremiah asks why the wicked's way prospers, using Hebrew צלח. This contrast shows that prospering in the corresponding root's Hebrew use does not itself establish righteous deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a960a86bc731a35b375a", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.34", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "no longer teach neighbor and brother to know God", "note": "The commentary gives believers a continuing duty to urge one another in truth; Jeremiah imagines a new covenant in which neighbor and brother no longer teach each other to know God because all know him, a particular limit on mutual instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d4e64843a5486057d51", "ref": "WLC:Jer.9.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth disappears from dealings", "note": "The commentary's remedy is mutual transmission of truth; Jeremiah depicts the inverse society, where people bend tongues for lies and do not advance by truth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f81e8adc519b01abf137", "ref": "WLC:Jer.9.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brother deceives brother", "note": "The commentary moves from estranged rivals to faithful companions; Jeremiah warns that every brother supplants and every neighbor slanders, a concrete collapse of the trusted bond."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05b9acdb027751bc46ba", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew אנוש, mortal human in trouble", "note": "The commentary labels الإنسان under ء ن س; the table notes BDB's Arabic-cognate note for Hebrew אנוש, and Job describes this mortal human as short-lived and full of trouble, sharpening the ayah's human-in-loss claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1f802985747d7a8de99", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew אנוש, mortal and troubled", "note": "The commentary's source label for الإنسان is ء ن س; the table notes BDB's Arabic-cognate note for אנוש, and Job's short-lived, trouble-filled mortal gives a specific Hebrew use of the human-in-loss image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8c0d2d9c6168c83ac74", "ref": "WLC:Job.19.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brothers and acquaintances estranged from Job", "note": "Job says his kin have withdrawn and his acquaintances are strangers; this gives a stark counter-image to the commentary's claim that human loss is answered by bonds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c6fa630dd4026adb80d", "ref": "WLC:Job.19.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "intimate friends turn against Job", "note": "Job says those he loved have turned against him; this preserves the contrary reading that close friendship can collapse into aversion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5cdd870b1d2fa61aa8d9", "ref": "WLC:Job.19.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "beloved friends turn against sufferer", "note": "The commentary's lost affection leaves a human isolated; Job laments that intimate friends abhor him and those he loved have turned against him, an inverse of patient companionship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9df2a4860ead2fe38b83", "ref": "WLC:Job.2.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "friends remain beside Job in grief", "note": "Job's friends sit with him on the ground for seven days without speaking; their patient presence fits the commentary's companion standing beside a group and answers isolation through company."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db55d6303806b9b74570", "ref": "WLC:Job.4.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س / Hebrew אנש (table sound correspondence)", "note": "The commentary's disputants each claim right while insan remains vulnerable; Job's speaker asks whether mortal enosh can be righteous before God. The proposed root correspondence illuminates mortal limitation, not the commentary's intimacy sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0251bb596d8ab6ca48a", "ref": "WLC:Job.42.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pray for disputing friends", "note": "The commentary asks that opposed people exchange good rather than contend over right; after Job's hard dispute with his friends, he prays for them and his fortunes are restored."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06758123117a0dffe8fa", "ref": "WLC:Job.42.11", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kin come to comfort Job", "note": "The commentary's isolated sufferer is rejoined by people who stand with him; Job's brothers, sisters and former acquaintances eat with him and comfort him after his ordeal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-08ccd83157d4e5e564f6", "ref": "WLC:Job.6.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "loyal care due to a suffering friend", "note": "The commentary's patient companion stands beside someone at risk of loss; Job complains that a suffering person should receive steadfast kindness from a friend rather than abandonment."}
