{"connection_id": "BC-034ff7f52d69d483451a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.11.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "people speak to one another to build together", "note": "The repeated mutual speech coordinates Babel's brickmaking; unlike the commentary's counsel toward haqq, this is a counter-example of solidarity directed toward a self-exalting project."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3302c06b5fb82c0bf5fa", "ref": "WLC:Gen.11.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shared speech is confused so people cannot understand one another", "note": "The Babel scene reverses the commentary's image of people passing counsel between them: collective speech becomes unintelligible and the group is broken apart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f91fc1b98a35779f96b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.13.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strife stopped because the parties are kin", "note": "Abram asks that there be no strife between him and Lot because they are brothers; kinship removes the repulsion that the commentary says sulh repairs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8555f1988bc58145e267", "ref": "WLC:Gen.13.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strife between kin", "note": "Abram asks Lot to end the quarrel between their herdsmen because they are brothers; it gives a concrete family scene for replacing contention with an acknowledged bond."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7578273c32480b027f2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Esau embraces the brother he had opposed", "note": "Esau runs to Jacob, embraces him, and they weep; the estranged brothers' bodily welcome gives a particular scene of hostility yielding to closeness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13aa0e94d15b7329c278", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Esau embraces Jacob", "note": "The commentary's enemy becomes a close companion; after Jacob fears Esau, Esau runs to embrace and kiss him, giving a narrative instance of hostile distance becoming kinship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f52ba1225b3cb0dc4f63", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brothers cannot speak peaceably", "note": "The commentary begins with estrangement that peace must remove; Joseph's brothers hate him and cannot speak to him in peace, making the broken bond audible in their speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d97473cd8755e10388e7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.4.9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "am I my brother's keeper", "note": "The commentary answers the solitary human's loss through responsibility for companions; Cain evades responsibility for his murdered brother with this question, giving the stark opposite of mutual care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f0f0bc14aa08d46c20d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joseph embraces and talks with brothers", "note": "The commentary's separated kin are joined through interpersonal exchange; after revealing himself, Joseph kisses his brothers and they speak with him, reversing their earlier alienation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-66a7672dd31af29c9d5f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joseph tells frightened brothers not to grieve over their betrayal", "note": "Joseph reframes their sale of him as God's means of preserving life; the speech releases his brothers from fear and turns a contested wrong toward reconciliation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20b1035bc2acd0585392", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brothers ask Joseph for forgiveness", "note": "The commentary's peace removes the fear that divided relatives; Joseph's brothers ask him to forgive their evil, and Joseph weeps when they speak, placing repaired kinship after wrongdoing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b32cbbb2f19c897bd7b6", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joseph comforts his brothers and speaks kindly", "note": "After the brothers fear revenge again, Joseph reassures and comforts them; this is a necessary continuation showing reconciliation maintained through compassionate speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9bdb2b5fa73431bb0a8e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.21", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "comfort and kindly speech after fear", "note": "The commentary's frightened separation becomes close companionship; Joseph tells his brothers not to fear, promises provision for their children, and speaks to their hearts after their plea for forgiveness."}
