{"connection_id": "BC-198eb1fd9f28303f690c", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "frank reproof of a neighbor", "note": "The commentary's reciprocal counsel gives another person what he needs; this law requires reproof of a fellow rather than hidden hatred, so truthful correction serves a continuing relationship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5468826a9359a90362cb", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.18", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "neighbor-love instead of vengeance or grudge", "note": "The verse forbids vengeance and resentment between members of the people before commanding love of neighbor; it gives the commentary's move from conflict to closeness a concrete ethical form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18bd00d39b81f4cbadab", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.18", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "love of neighbor after grievance", "note": "The commentary replaces mutual grievance with affection; this neighboring law forbids vengeance and grudges against one's people and commands love of neighbor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd7ab5aa5736f17ed50c", "ref": "WLC:Mal.2.10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one Father and treachery between brothers", "note": "Malachi asks why people betray one another if they share one creator and ancestral covenant; this grounds the commentary's kinship bond in a moral claim against division."}
{"connection_id": "BC-23b2c51115dee6b7af59", "ref": "WLC:Mal.2.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth and peace in priestly speech", "note": "The commentary joins haqq to reconciliation; Malachi recalls true instruction in Levi's mouth and his walking with God in peace and uprightness, linking reliable teaching with peaceful conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca0652e56ce3c24b8b6c", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "those who fear God speak to one another", "note": "Their shared speech is heard and remembered by God; the verse gives the commentary's mutual exchange a communal act with divine recognition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d94e96d216c06a3ed60", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "those who fear God speak with one another", "note": "The commentary stresses the reciprocal verb, each giving and receiving counsel; here the God-fearing speak to one another, and their shared speech is remembered before God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55924bc1d67f0baa437e", "ref": "WLC:Mic.7.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mistrust of close companion", "note": "The commentary's reconciliation reverses fear and withdrawal; Micah says not to trust a neighbor or intimate friend, depicting social breakdown at the level of close ties."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2253a2b161d94419ae6e", "ref": "WLC:Num.11.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses unable to carry the people alone", "note": "Moses says the people's burden is too heavy for him alone; this gives a concrete counterpart to the commentary's solitary human in loss before its turn toward shared support."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ab9073f90a0e9ac315f7", "ref": "WLC:Num.11.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "elders share Moses's burden", "note": "God puts some of Moses's spirit on the elders so they bear the people with him; the scene makes the commentary's move from lone loss to a group that carries together concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de098d2e8fb6fd301d86", "ref": "WLC:Prov.10.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hatred stirs strife; love covers offenses", "note": "The commentary reverses contention into affection; this proverb gives both movements in one line, with hatred stirring up disputes and love covering wrongs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae1f9f4988d3f57844ff", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wise person listens to counsel", "note": "The fool thinks his way right in his own eyes, but the wise listen to counsel; this directly answers the commentary's image of each side claiming haqq for itself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20e97f36ac3310b66d27", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.20", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "counselors of peace have joy", "note": "The commentary makes mutual counsel an instrument of peace; this proverb opposes deceit in hearts that devise evil to joy among advisers who counsel peace."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87fc32b5829a151783d6", "ref": "WLC:Prov.15.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gentle answer turns away anger", "note": "The commentary says peace removes repulsion; Proverbs identifies a gentle reply as one way to turn wrath aside, though it describes one speaker's action rather than reciprocal counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7639c760aabb8009196b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.15.18", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "slow anger calms contention", "note": "The patient person quiets a quarrel, while the hot-tempered one stirs it; this gives sabr a direct social effect in ending strife."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13364f5bf6061a2aede6", "ref": "WLC:Prov.15.18", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "patience stills a quarrel", "note": "The commentary makes sabr the way from enmity to closeness; this proverb says a hot-tempered person stirs strife while one slow to anger quiets contention."}
