+{"root":"ء ن س","decision":"used","hebrew":["אנש"],"reason":"BDB explicitly notes an Arabic cognate for Hebrew אנש; WLC:Ps.8.5 has אֱנוֹשׁ for mortal human and WLC:Dan.2.10 has Aramaic אֲנָשׁ for human. Neither passage itself expresses the Arabic ünsiyet sense. Hebrew/Aramaic אנס denotes compulsion or oppression (Esth.1.8; Dan.4.6), so it is excluded.","paragraphs":[26],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"root":"ح ق ق","decision":"used","hebrew":["חקק"],"reason":"Sound correspondence only, with no lexicon cognate attestation. Hebrew חקק means inscribe or decree across WLC; Prov.8.15 links a decree to justice but Isa.10.1 condemns iniquitous decrees. It does not itself mean the shared truth of Qur'an 103:3.","paragraphs":[26],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-002"]}
+{"root":"ص ب ر","decision":"false_friend","hebrew":["צבר"],"reason":"Sound correspondence only. Hebrew צבר means heap up grain or possessions (Gen.41.49; Ps.39.7), not endurance or a companion who remains beside others; its eight WLC occurrences yield no fitting social patience scene.","paragraphs":[26,28],"annotations":[]}
+{"root":"ص ل ح","decision":"no_qualifying_parallel","hebrew":["צלח"],"reason":"Sound correspondence only. Hebrew צלח and Biblical Aramaic צלח mean advance or prosper, including the wicked way prospering in Jer.12.1 and elders prospering while building in Ezra.6.14. Neither expresses reconciliation or righteous deeds in this section; the root's pots/dishes are unrelated forms.","paragraphs":[26,28],"annotations":[]}
+{"root":"ع ص ر","decision":"false_friend","hebrew":["עצר"],"reason":"Sound correspondence only. Hebrew עצר means restrain or convene; its festival assembly is explicit in Deut.16.8, Joel.1.14 and Amos.5.21, and self-restraint in Prov.25.28. None denotes the Arabic page's affection or kinship, nor Qur'an 103:1's time oath.","paragraphs":[26],"annotations":[]}
+{"root":"و ص ي","decision":"no_hebrew_cognate","hebrew":[],"reason":"The supplied correspondence table and local lexicon find no Hebrew or Biblical Aramaic root with corresponding consonants. Mutual counsel is assessed through wording and motif, not a claimed lexical cognate.","paragraphs":[26,27],"annotations":[]}
*** End Patch"));

