+{"connection_id":"BC-f179e15e5e809ca109c0","ref":"Sifra, Kedoshim 4:12","status":"unavailable","reason":"The Akiva interpretation lacks a local Sifra text for verification.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-92edb6af7933739df897","ref":"Sirach 28:2","status":"unavailable","reason":"Sirach 28:2 is absent from the local snapshot, so the neighbor-forgiveness wording cannot be confirmed.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6b25bf0d3334260d837a","ref":"Sirach 28:2","status":"unavailable","reason":"The second Sirach forgiveness proposal remains unverified without an available text.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a22448edd5944abd532a","ref":"Sirach 28:8","status":"unavailable","reason":"The strife-and-anger saying has no local Sirach witness for quotation.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d92929454cf2a6c5bb0e","ref":"Sirach 6:14","status":"unavailable","reason":"The faithful-friend shelter metaphor cannot be verified from a local Sirach text.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6dbce92f38375abf563f","ref":"Sirach 6:14","status":"unavailable","reason":"This repeated shelter-and-treasure proposal lacks a held original or translation in this run.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a16b37b90cdfa86e8ebc","ref":"Sirach 6:16","status":"unavailable","reason":"The friend's healing-medicine wording cannot be checked against the frozen local sources.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d560dbc7349e7216e7bd","ref":"Testament of Gad 6","status":"unavailable","reason":"No local Testament of Gad text verifies the chapter's peaceful-speech wording or verse division.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8d1038fab728fc656f24","ref":"WLC:1Kgs.12.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Israel rejects Rehoboam and leaves, but this single verse does not say counsel was rejected; it is a national schism, not mutual truth-giving.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:1Kgs.12.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b6da3628545f988abbbe","ref":"WLC:1Kgs.12.7","status":"rejected","reason":"The elders advise a king to serve and speak kindly, a ruler-subject relation rather than advice exchanged between peers.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:1Kgs.12.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-80a0b0c7c3ab63ababbf","ref":"WLC:1Sam.18.1","status":"rejected","reason":"Jonathan's attachment to David illustrates chosen friendship, but the WLC has ketiv ויאהבו with a separate qere; it does not link friendship to mutual counsel or the Arabic root ء ن س.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:1Sam.18.1"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-385a700feb24feb5efa4","ref":"WLC:1Sam.20.42","status":"rejected","reason":"A peace oath binds Jonathan and David across separation, but it does not narrate a quarrel between them or its resolution.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:1Sam.20.42"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-76641c2d33546e1e2516","ref":"WLC:1Sam.25.33","status":"rejected","reason":"David thanks Abigail for keeping him from bloodguilt, a one-way intervention before violence, not reciprocal counsel among opposed parties.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:1Sam.25.33"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ffa5e69b6af612f34ed4","ref":"WLC:2Chr.28.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Clothing and feeding released captives illustrates wartime mercy; Exod 23:5 and Prov 25:21 state care for a named enemy more directly.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:2Chr.28.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c8b910aecc274b15bddb","ref":"WLC:2Kgs.6.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Elisha feeds and releases Aramean captives and the raids cease, but the scene concerns state hostilities rather than personal reciprocal counsel; Exod 23:5 supplies a direct ethical command.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:2Kgs.6.23"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2979e85bbbcf1b352af1","ref":"WLC:Amos.5.21","status":"rejected","reason":"The pointed Hebrew בְּעַצְּרֹֽתֵיכֶֽם means your assemblies; the proposed ع ص ر link has only sound correspondence and no shared affection or endurance sense.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Amos.5.21"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-895f0444f79269ce0a63","ref":"WLC:Dan.2.10","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse declares no human can reveal the royal dream, but the companion-based resolution is in later verses; 2:10 itself does not make the proposed social contrast.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.2.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1da09d9def33af2cf406","ref":"WLC:Dan.2.10","status":"accepted","reason":"Aramaic אֲנָשׁ explicitly names a human, a useful sound-corresponding lexical contrast with the Arabic page's insan; it does not attest friendship.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.8.5","WLC:Dan.2.10"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d678edd91b94a5829215","ref":"WLC:Dan.2.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Daniel tells companions the matter, but this verse does not yet contain their shared prayer; its crisis response differs from mutual counsel in 103:3.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.2.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c2974e7b20d639e7c738","ref":"WLC:Dan.7.13","status":"rejected","reason":"The Aramaic human-like figure is visionary and exalted, not the ordinary vulnerable person or close friend of paragraph 26; the lexical evidence is better from Dan 2:10.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.7.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-098842c4cee950dffe4d","ref":"WLC:Deut.1.12","status":"rejected","reason":"Moses cannot bear troubles and disputes alone, but the next verses assign judges; this is administrative burden sharing, whereas Eccl 4:10 directly contrasts isolated person and companion.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.1.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7b63830f723184e5554e","ref":"WLC:Deut.1.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Choosing wise leaders distributes adjudication, not mutual advice among all parties in paragraph 27.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.1.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-41c4834b1f18da332fcc","ref":"WLC:Deut.1.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Judges must hear brothers and judge justly, but the communication is between disputants and appointed judges; Prov 18:17 more precisely tests rival claims.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.1.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c10e67e3b42d2655fc75","ref":"WLC:Deut.15.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Opening one's hand to a poor brother is a direct charity law; paragraph 27's poor-feeding mention illustrates failed exhortation, while Prov 25:21 uniquely combines food and the enemy relation.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.15.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9227260487c125ab1cb8","ref":"WLC:Deut.16.8","status":"rejected","reason":"WLC עֲצֶ֨רֶת֙ means festival assembly; consonantal resemblance to Arabic ع ص ر does not support its affection or kinship sense.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.16.8"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-28696351ef9eb24afc85","ref":"WLC:Deut.16.8","status":"rejected","reason":"The sound-corresponding עצר form denotes a mandated festival gathering and rest from work, not the Arabic page's love bond; no semantic bridge is established.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.16.8"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-17677c8586f40b89ee0c","ref":"WLC:Deut.25.1","status":"rejected","reason":"The law sends disputants to judges who declare righteous and wicked; Prov 18:17 better captures why a party's initial claim of right needs scrutiny.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.25.1"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e2767b8e5b9964e27981","ref":"WLC:Deut.25.1","status":"rejected","reason":"This duplicates the litigants-and-judges scene; it concerns a formal verdict rather than the interparty movement toward reciprocal counsel.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.25.1"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-70714d8999749696d225","ref":"WLC:Deut.6.20","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse has חֻקִּים, statutes, not the root letters חקק as a surface form; its child asks about laws, a generational teaching setting unlike mutual haqq between disputants.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.6.20"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-dc5d9994020a268aa16f","ref":"WLC:Eccl.4.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew directly contrasts a companion lifting a fallen person with the solitary fallen person lacking a helper.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.4.9","WLC:Eccl.4.10","WLC:Eccl.4.12"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b05acf873b0da3e257b4","ref":"WLC:Eccl.4.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The same companion-lifting wording supports the page's contrast of isolation and mutual aid.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.4.9","WLC:Eccl.4.10","WLC:Eccl.4.12"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c0dc6c88f0c591786212","ref":"WLC:Eccl.4.12","status":"accepted","reason":"Two withstand an attacker and a threefold cord resists tearing; the linked note expresses this resilience of joined people.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.4.9","WLC:Eccl.4.10","WLC:Eccl.4.12"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4aa311eb1f17add0e4fa","ref":"WLC:Eccl.4.12","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse's two-person resistance makes togetherness protective, extending the companion-lifting scene of 4:10.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.4.9","WLC:Eccl.4.10","WLC:Eccl.4.12"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-79bf6cad6ba556b96180","ref":"WLC:Eccl.4.9","status":"accepted","reason":"Two are better than one because their shared work has a good return, setting up the specific aid and strength imagery in 4:10-12.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.4.9","WLC:Eccl.4.10","WLC:Eccl.4.12"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fe4beec76faa8f9808b5","ref":"WLC:Exod.23.4","status":"rejected","reason":"Returning an enemy's stray animal is practical mercy but less tied to the paragraph's burden-and-food imagery than Exod 23:5 and Prov 25:21.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.23.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4734bb9d47ebfd89e992","ref":"WLC:Exod.23.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew explicitly orders help with the burdened donkey of one who hates you, a concrete act across enmity.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.23.5","WLC:Prov.25.21"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f14e3bb5810ffead4210","ref":"WLC:Exod.33.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Moses' face-to-face talk with God is likened to human friend-talk, but the scene is divine-human and does not show peer reconciliation or the ء ن س lexical family.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.33.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-560e42a750a3c454531d","ref":"WLC:Ezek.37.22","status":"rejected","reason":"Judah and Israel becoming one nation is national restoration under one king, a different scale and mechanism from the paragraph's interpersonal peace and counsel.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.37.22"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-57362345d682a92f1980","ref":"WLC:Ezra.6.14","status":"rejected","reason":"Aramaic וּמַצְלְחִ֔ין means prospering in temple building; sound correspondence to ص ل ح does not establish reconciliation or righteous deeds.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ezra.6.14"],"annotations":[]}

