+{"connection_id":"BC-1799d11902743d91024e","ref":"WLC:Prov.8.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew חקק verb appears beside צדק, showing a just decree use that can be contrasted with Isa 10:1's unjust decrees.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Prov.8.15","WLC:Isa.10.1"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d8886832829f4d470bc5","ref":"WLC:Ps.119.165","status":"rejected","reason":"The psalm promises peace to lovers of Torah but does not speak of peace between disputants; Zech 8:16 ties truth to interpersonal peace judgment.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.119.165"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-939de8c580bfe006eae1","ref":"WLC:Ps.119.63","status":"rejected","reason":"The speaker is a companion of commandment-keepers, but the verse neither uses the proposed ء ن س cognate nor depicts counsel through which friendship forms.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.119.63"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-445a7b446c6c851ffb9c","ref":"WLC:Ps.120.7","status":"rejected","reason":"The speaker says peace while others want war, a genuine limit on reciprocal success; it lacks the positive repair process discussed in paragraph 28 and cannot itself establish mutual counsel.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.120.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5cc8bb8c974fd9a1515c","ref":"WLC:Ps.122.9","status":"rejected","reason":"The candidate mislocates the brothers-and-companions peace wish: that wording is WLC Ps 122:8; verse 9 seeks Jerusalem's good for God's house.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.122.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0f40565636f5204d47a6","ref":"WLC:Ps.133.1","status":"rejected","reason":"Brothers dwelling together is a communal ideal but does not show formerly opposed parties or exchange of truthful counsel; Eccl 4:10 gives the solitary-helper contrast.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.133.1"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-cecb9a329f235c57f03b","ref":"WLC:Ps.133.1","status":"rejected","reason":"The repeated unity image lacks a conflict or reciprocal aid; it would add only a general pleasant-togetherness example to the section.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.133.1"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-71896d76a07cf9e9b86f","ref":"WLC:Ps.34.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Seeking and pursuing peace is a broad command without parties or method; Matt 5:24 supplies a concrete prior grievance and act of reconciliation.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.34.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-94104aebf8fdec718777","ref":"WLC:Ps.34.15","status":"rejected","reason":"This repeats the active-peace command, but the verse's generality adds less than Exod 23:5's aid across hostility or Prov 15:18's patient quieting of strife.","paragraphs":[28],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.34.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-76deb7725c227f487ff0","ref":"WLC:Ps.45.5","status":"rejected","reason":"Hebrew צְלַ֬ח is a royal advance while riding for truth and righteousness; sound correspondence alone does not support Arabic ص ل ح as reconciliation.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.45.5"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-82d6f4a0085df04f1c48","ref":"WLC:Ps.55.14","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse names an intimate acquaintance with אֱנוֹשׁ, but betrayal is established only by surrounding Psalm 55:13,15,21; it is not a cognate use of friendship.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.55.14"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-55f6c82f113a8a7e0764","ref":"WLC:Ps.8.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The WLC has אֱנוֹשׁ for the human God remembers, grounding the BDB-noted Hebrew-Arabic lexical connection without claiming the Hebrew verse means friendship.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.8.5","WLC:Dan.2.10"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4bb917e3bc108ee2fe87","ref":"WLC:Ps.8.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The repeated candidate's mortal-human wording is present in WLC and helps distinguish human vulnerability from the Arabic page's social sense.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.8.5","WLC:Dan.2.10"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0c28fa85630b263e6cb5","ref":"WLC:Ps.8.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The root connection is lexically plausible by BDB note; Psalm 8:5 attests human, not intimacy, and the annotation states that distinction.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.8.5","WLC:Dan.2.10"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-47813a2259f07f5ddd81","ref":"WLC:Ps.85.11","status":"rejected","reason":"The psalm says kindness and truth meet, righteousness and peace kiss; this is a poetic personification, not a direct scene of people exchanging haqq as Zech 8:16 is.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.85.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-68652e1e292db0fbca6c","ref":"WLC:Ps.85.11","status":"rejected","reason":"The repeated truth-peace kissing image is evocative but does not specify human speakers or counsel; Zech 8:16 does.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.85.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-09086957d4d74bc45b0d","ref":"WLC:Ps.90.3","status":"rejected","reason":"The Hebrew אֱנוֹשׁ denotes the mortal returning to dust, but adds no new social nuance beyond Ps 8:5's selected cognate witness.","paragraphs":[26],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.90.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-efeb2de10227dfbc9651","ref":"WLC:Ruth.1.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Ruth chooses Naomi's people and God, a moving loyalty scene, but there are no rival truth claims or reciprocal counsel between the two in this verse.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Ruth.1.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b5ed69931263575ff773","ref":"WLC:Zech.7.10","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse forbids oppressing vulnerable people and plotting against a brother; Zech 8:16 more closely joins truthful speech and peace judgment in one instruction.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.7.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-89cba24d9f0928ae0d5b","ref":"WLC:Zech.7.9","status":"rejected","reason":"True judgment and mutual mercy are close but do not include speech between neighbors; Zech 8:16 states that act along with peaceful judgment.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.7.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f7d8f2f4d79ff2e4fe8e","ref":"WLC:Zech.7.9","status":"rejected","reason":"The repeated mutual-mercy proposal does not add to 8:16's closer truth-speech and peace command; 7:9 concerns judges' conduct and compassion.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.7.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-559fe66a3605b94efd99","ref":"WLC:Zech.8.10","status":"rejected","reason":"A past time without peace sets neighbor against neighbor, but is retrospective context; 8:16 gives the operative truth-and-peace instruction.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.8.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ae7b0e6824ceac43b6c4","ref":"WLC:Zech.8.16","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew joins each person speaking truth to a neighbor with truth-and-peace judgments at the gate.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.8.16","WLC:Zech.8.19"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-99523738cdbdc6b92e01","ref":"WLC:Zech.8.16","status":"accepted","reason":"The repeated candidate is verified: interpersonal truth speech and peace judgment are in the same verse.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.8.16","WLC:Zech.8.19"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8741bb21c4ef98baf9dd","ref":"WLC:Zech.8.17","status":"rejected","reason":"The ban on plotting harm and false oaths continues 8:16, but is a negative restraint rather than another distinct truth-and-peace motif.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.8.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ef175343ba0e67e277eb","ref":"WLC:Zech.8.19","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse explicitly commands love of truth and peace, completing 8:16's neighbor-speech and peace judgment.","paragraphs":[27],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.8.16","WLC:Zech.8.19"],"annotations":["S103-TEV-MTF-003"]}

